#!/usr/bin/bash
# Decide whether this boot counts as a success.
#
# Ordered before boot-complete.target and required by it, which is the seam
# systemd provides for exactly this: systemd-bless-boot.service is ordered AFTER
# boot-complete.target and Requires= it, so a failure here means the boot loader
# entry keeps its decremented counter. Three such boots and sd-boot stops offering
# that entry first, and the machine comes up on the previous deployment instead.
#
# Nothing here rolls anything back. That is deliberate: the rollback is the boot
# loader declining to pick an entry it has already given three chances, which
# works when userspace is too broken to run a rollback command at all — which is
# the case this exists for.
#
# ## What counts as healthy, and why it is not the same test the floor uses
#
# Someone can sign in: the sign-in screen is drawn and stays, and a keyboard
# or touch screen reaches it. A system that started is not that. An update
# whose keyboard driver is broken starts, shows its greeter and is useless;
# blessed at that point it would be the machine's default for good. Nothing
# here waits for a person, though: a machine nobody signs in to, or one that
# loses power at its sign-in screen, has done everything the update controls,
# and holding the verdict for a person would leave an unattended machine on
# trial and roll back a working update after a few power cuts. So, in order:
#
#   1. The greeter is on the screen and stays there:
#      * greetd is active;
#      * the greeter running now has presented a frame (tilo-greeter's own
#        startup record, which its first presented frame completes), or a
#        person is already signed in through greetd (a first boot's autologin);
#      * and it holds: for SETTLE seconds greetd is not restarted and the
#        greeter keeps presenting.
#      This part has a deadline, because a greeter that cannot draw will not
#      start drawing later, and its verdict is worth having in the journal.
#   2. Input reaches the sign-in screen: the kiosk compositor gave the
#      greeter's window a keyboard or a touch screen. It advertises on its
#      seat the keyboard and touch devices it has opened, and the greeter
#      records the first such seat it sees (GREETER_INPUT; a mouse alone
#      records nothing). A person signed in through greetd settles this too,
#      whichever comes first. This part shares part 1's
#      deadline, with at least INPUT_WAIT seconds after the greeter held: a
#      device that is plugged in is opened when the compositor starts, or
#      when it appears.
#
# A machine whose disk asks for a passphrase reaches its greeter only after
# someone typed it; with TPM unlock it gets there by itself. A boot that never
# gets past the unlock prompt never runs this, keeps its decremented counter
# and has spent its try, which is right: that is the update whose keyboard
# does not work at unlock. The verdict blocks boot-complete.target and
# systemd-bless-boot meanwhile, and anything that must not act on an unproven
# boot (tilo-rollback-confirm) is ordered after the blessing.
#
# ## A node is judged by what it serves
#
# A machine provisioned as a node (docs/node-machine.md) has no sign-in screen
# at all: it serves a person's rooms to their web pages, and its liveness is
# that it answers. Units being active is not that. An update that leaves the
# relay up and unable to answer — a certificate it cannot load, a listener that
# bound and wedged, a session key its pages no longer trust — is exactly what a
# rollback is for, and the greeter such a machine does not have would never have
# said so. So it is judged by where it says its own liveness is
# (`/etc/tilo/node/health`): `tilo-node-readiness` reads the record the relay
# published (the boot it started in, the endpoint it serves on, its session's
# signature over both) and asks the host answering there for its own identity
# (crates/hosting/tilo-relay-client/src/readiness.rs, `tilo-relay
# --readiness-out`). The session beside the relay is up when systemctl says so,
# which is what `set -e` in its start-up means: that is native readiness, the
# node's apps running.
#
# A node announces itself with a file rather than being guessed at from the
# units it has: a dev VM is a node while a persona runs the persona suite and a
# desktop again afterwards, and provisioning that took the greeter away would
# otherwise take the desktop's own health check with it.
#
# `greetd is active` alone is not even part 1. greetd has Restart=, so it is
# active between restarts, and a greeter that cannot draw (a broken GPU driver,
# cage or tilo-greeter) exits, takes greetd with it and is back a few seconds
# later, for ever. Judged by is-active alone, such a boot was blessed on its
# first attempt, kept no try counter, and the machine never fell back: a black
# or flickering screen for good. Restarts before the greeter first presents are
# not held against the boot (a slow display probe can cost one); restarts after
# it are, for the whole settle window.
#
# It is deliberately NOT `systemctl is-system-running` = running, which
# /usr/libexec/tilo-rollback-common uses for a different decision. That check gates
# whether the fallback is *destroyed*, where being strict is right: refuse, keep
# the fallback, lose nothing. This one gates whether the current generation is
# kept, where the same strictness is a trap — one unrelated failed unit would
# fail every boot of every generation, walk the whole retention window down, and
# leave the machine with nothing it is willing to boot. Failed units are reported
# here and do not by themselves condemn the boot.
set -uo pipefail

log()  { printf 'tilo-boot-health: %s\n' "$*"; }
warn() { printf 'tilo-boot-health: WARNING: %s\n' "$*" >&2; }

# How long part 1 waits for the greeter. greetd starting is not instantaneous and the
# boot-complete transaction runs as soon as the default target settles, so
# polling is the only correct shape — ordering After=greetd.service would make a
# hung greetd hang the assessment instead of failing it.
DEADLINE="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_TIMEOUT:-}"
# How long the greeter has to keep presenting, with greetd never restarted,
# before the boot counts: longer than one crash cycle of a greeter that cannot
# draw. wlroots waits 10 s for a display device before cage gives up, so such a
# greetd is back every ~11 s.
SETTLE="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_SETTLE:-20}"
POLL="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_POLL:-2}"
# tilo-greeter's startup record; /usr/libexec/tilo-cage-session points the
# greeter at it. Each greeter process replaces the file from its first mark on,
# so it tells what the greeter running now has done, never what an earlier one
# did. /run is visible here; the greeter's /tmp is not (PrivateTmp=).
GREETER_RECORD="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_GREETER_RECORD:-/run/tilo-greeter/startup-metrics.json}"
# How long part 2 waits at least, once the greeter held, for a keyboard or
# touch screen to reach it.
INPUT_WAIT="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_INPUT_WAIT:-30}"
# The greeter's record of the keyboard or touch screen it was given
# (crates/session/greeter/src/seat_input.rs). Like the startup record it is in
# the greeter user's own directory and describes the greeter running now.
GREETER_INPUT="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_GREETER_INPUT:-/run/tilo-greeter/input}"

# A machine with no greeter is not a broken desktop, it is a different machine —
# the probe image the boot chain is tested on, an installer medium, a headless
# build. Assessing it against a unit it does not have would fail every boot for a
# reason that has nothing to do with health. TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_UNIT names what to
# wait for, so a machine that is not the desktop can say what its own liveness is.
#
# An always-on board (os/dev/install-board-layer.sh) masks greetd; its liveness
# is its board session for the account the disk was made with. That session
# waits for the user manager, Wi-Fi and the first clock sync before it is
# active, about five minutes at worst, so the board is allowed two such
# attempts. A board with no account yet is judged by multi-user.target.
board_marker="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_BOARD_MARKER:-/usr/share/tilo/board-headless-session}"
board_wants="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_WANTS:-/etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants}"
# Where a node says how it is alive: TILO_NODE_HEALTH_UNIT is the unit that has
# to be active, TILO_NODE_READINESS the file its relay publishes and
# TILO_NODE_SESSION the session beside it. Only this one file path is
# configured; what it names does the judging.
node_marker="${TILO_NODE_HEALTH_MARKER:-/etc/tilo/node/health}"
if [ -z "${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_UNIT:-}" ] && [ -f "$board_marker" ]; then
  TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_UNIT=multi-user.target
  for link in "$board_wants"/tilo-board-headless-session@*.service; do
    [ -L "$link" ] || continue
    TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_UNIT="${link##*/}"
    DEADLINE="${DEADLINE:-660}"
    break
  done
fi
# The lines of a node's state a person can read from the serial console.
node_final_state() {
  local unit
  for unit in "$UNIT" ${TILO_NODE_SESSION:-}; do
    systemctl status --no-pager --lines=20 "$unit" 2>&1 \
      | sed 's/^/tilo-boot-health:   /' >&2
  done
  systemctl --failed --no-legend --plain 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/tilo-boot-health:   failed: /' >&2
}

# Why the node does not answer, in the words of what a person would look at.
readiness_reason() {
  case "$1" in
    *"does not answer"*)
      echo "it accepted connections and answered nothing, so the relay is up and serving" \
        "no page" ;;
    *"another host answers"*)
      echo "another host answered on the address the relay published" ;;
    *"published no readiness"*)
      echo "it published nothing while starting, so it never reached serving" ;;
    *"not this node's readiness"*)
      echo "the record there was written by another node, or before this session was" \
        "installed" ;;
    *)
      echo "what it published could not be read" ;;
  esac
}

# Exits with the verdict on a node: it answers at the address it published, as
# the host whose key it named, behind the certificate it pins, and the session
# that runs its apps is running.
#
# The answer is not enough on its own. It says a relay is listening; the record
# in front of it says which node published it and in which boot, and its
# session device key's signature says the record is not one left behind. Both
# together are what a page checks before it opens a room, so that is what the
# boot that keeps the update checks.
judge_node() {
  local end=$(( $(date +%s) + ${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_NODE_DEADLINE:-180} )) reason answered
  while :; do
    if ! systemctl is-active --quiet "$UNIT"; then
      reason="$UNIT did not come up"
    elif [ -n "${TILO_NODE_SESSION:-}" ] && ! systemctl is-active --quiet "$TILO_NODE_SESSION"; then
      reason="$TILO_NODE_SESSION did not come up, so the node's apps are not there"
    elif answered="$(TILO_NODE_READINESS_PATIENCE=$(( end - $(date +%s) )) \
        TILO_NODE_READINESS="$TILO_NODE_READINESS" \
        TILO_NODE_SESSION_ENV="${TILO_NODE_SESSION_ENV:-}" \
        "${TILO_NODE_READINESS_PROGRAM:-/usr/libexec/tilo-node-readiness}" 2>&1)"; then
      log "$answered"
      if [ -n "${TILO_NODE_SESSION:-}" ]; then
        log "$TILO_NODE_SESSION is running beside it — this boot is good"
      else
        log "this boot is good"
      fi
      report_failed_units
      exit 0
    else
      reason="$(readiness_reason "$answered")"
    fi
    if [ "$(date +%s)" -ge "$end" ]; then
      warn "$reason. This boot does not count as a success, so the boot loader entry"
      warn "keeps its decremented try counter and the previous deployment becomes the"
      warn "default once the tries run out. What the node said was:"
      printf '%s\n' "$answered" | sed 's/^/tilo-boot-health:   /' >&2
      node_final_state
      exit 1
    fi
    sleep "$POLL"
  done
}

UNIT="${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_UNIT:-greetd.service}"
if [ -z "${TILO_BOOT_HEALTH_UNIT:-}" ] && [ -f "$node_marker" ]; then
  # What the node says about itself. A file that names neither its unit nor its
  # readiness is a provisioning mistake, and a check that cannot observe its
  # subject has to fail: the verdict under it decides whether an update is kept.
  # shellcheck disable=SC1090
  . "$node_marker"
  UNIT="${TILO_NODE_HEALTH_UNIT:-}"
  if [ -z "$UNIT" ] || [ -z "${TILO_NODE_READINESS:-}" ]; then
    warn "$node_marker names no TILO_NODE_HEALTH_UNIT or TILO_NODE_READINESS, so nothing"
    warn "here can tell whether this node works. This boot does not count as a success."
    warn "State was:"
    systemctl status --no-pager --lines=20 greetd.service 'tilo-node-*' 2>&1 \
      | sed 's/^/tilo-boot-health:   /' >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  judge_node
fi
if ! systemctl cat "$UNIT" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  log "$UNIT is not installed on this machine — using multi-user.target instead"
  UNIT="multi-user.target"
fi
# The greeter's wait is for its first presented frame, which on a machine that
# renders in software takes tens of seconds, not the instant greetd takes to
# become active. A longer wait costs a broken boot nothing: its try was spent
# when the boot loader chose the entry and only a verdict of good returns it, so
# a machine power-cycled before the verdict has failed its boot all the same.
if [ "$UNIT" = greetd.service ]; then
  DEADLINE="${DEADLINE:-180}"
else
  DEADLINE="${DEADLINE:-90}"
fi

report_failed_units() {
  local failed
  failed="$(systemctl --failed --no-legend --plain 2>/dev/null | wc -l)"
  if [ "${failed:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
    warn "$failed unit(s) failed on this boot; the session came up regardless, so"
    warn "this generation is not condemned. They are:"
    systemctl --failed --no-legend --plain 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/tilo-boot-health:   /' >&2
  fi
}

# A unit that has actually failed will not become active by waiting.
judge_failed() {
  warn "$UNIT FAILED. This boot does not count as a success, so the boot loader"
  warn "entry keeps its decremented try counter and the previous deployment"
  warn "becomes the default once the tries run out."
  systemctl status --no-pager --lines=20 "$UNIT" 2>&1 | sed 's/^/tilo-boot-health:   /' >&2
  exit 1
}

final_state() {
  systemctl status --no-pager --lines=20 "$UNIT" 2>&1 | sed 's/^/tilo-boot-health:   /' >&2
  systemctl --failed --no-legend --plain 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/tilo-boot-health:   failed: /' >&2
}

# A virtual machine made without any display device (the headless dev VMs under
# UTM, Hyper-V or WHPX, reached over SSH) has no screen for a greeter to present
# on; its greeter crash-loops on every boot of every generation, so asking for
# one would fail them all and walk the machine back through its deployments.
# Such a machine keeps the plain verdict, greetd active. Real hardware gets no
# such pass: there, no display device at all is what a broken GPU driver looks
# like, and exactly what this check has to catch.
plain=no
if [ "$UNIT" != greetd.service ]; then
  plain=yes
else
  drm_sysfs="${TILO_DRM_SYSFS:-/sys/class/drm}"
  displays="$(find "$drm_sysfs" -maxdepth 1 -name 'card[0-9]*' 2>/dev/null | wc -l)"
  virtualization="$(systemctl show -p Virtualization --value 2>/dev/null)"
  if [ "${displays:-0}" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "$virtualization" ] && [ "$virtualization" != none ]; then
    log "this is a $virtualization virtual machine with no display device, so no greeter can"
    log "present here; judging it by greetd being active"
    plain=yes
  fi
fi

end=$(( $(date +%s) + DEADLINE ))

# A person signed in through greetd.
person_session() {
  local session state
  for session in $(loginctl list-sessions --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk '{ print $1 }'); do
    [ "$(loginctl show-session "$session" -p Service --value 2>/dev/null)" = greetd ] || continue
    [ "$(loginctl show-session "$session" -p Class --value 2>/dev/null)" = user ] || continue
    state="$(loginctl show-session "$session" -p State --value 2>/dev/null)"
    [ "$state" = active ] || [ "$state" = online ] || continue
    signed_in="$session"
    return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# Sets `input_device` to the keyboard or touch screen the greeter recorded.
greeter_input() {
  local kind
  [ -r "$GREETER_INPUT" ] || return 1
  kind="$(head -c 32 "$GREETER_INPUT" | head -1)"
  case "$kind" in
    keyboard|touch) ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
  [ "$kind" = "touch" ] && input_device="touch screen" || input_device="keyboard"
  return 0
}

# Part 2: exits with the verdict once input reaches the sign-in screen, or a
# person signed in, or the wait is over.
await_input() {
  local input_end
  input_end=$(( $(date +%s) + INPUT_WAIT ))
  [ "$input_end" -ge "$end" ] || input_end="$end"
  while :; do
    if person_session; then
      log "session $signed_in is signed in through greetd — this boot is good"
      report_failed_units
      exit 0
    fi
    if greeter_input; then
      log "a $input_device reaches the sign-in screen — this boot is good"
      report_failed_units
      exit 0
    fi
    [ "$(date +%s)" -ge "$input_end" ] && break
    sleep "$POLL"
  done
  warn "no keyboard or touch screen reached the sign-in screen (the greeter recorded"
  warn "none in $GREETER_INPUT), and nobody signed in. Nobody can sign in, so this boot"
  warn "does not count as a success. State was:"
  final_state
  exit 1
}

if [ "$plain" = yes ]; then
  while :; do
    if systemctl is-active --quiet "$UNIT"; then
      log "$UNIT is active — this boot is good"
      report_failed_units
      exit 0
    fi
    systemctl is-failed --quiet "$UNIT" && judge_failed
    [ "$(date +%s)" -ge "$end" ] && break
    sleep "$POLL"
  done
  warn "$UNIT did not become active within ${DEADLINE}s. This boot does not count as"
  warn "a success. State was:"
  final_state
  exit 1
fi

greetd_restarts() {
  systemctl show -p NRestarts --value greetd.service 2>/dev/null
}
# Either value changing means the greeter that was presenting is gone: systemd
# restarted greetd, or something else replaced its main process.
greetd_generation() {
  systemctl show -p NRestarts -p MainPID --value greetd.service 2>/dev/null | tr '\n' ' '
}

# Sets `presenting_by` to what is on the screen.
greeter_presenting() {
  if [ -r "$GREETER_RECORD" ] && grep -q '"first-frame-presented"' "$GREETER_RECORD"; then
    presenting_by="its greeter has presented a frame"
    return 0
  fi
  local session
  for session in $(loginctl list-sessions --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk '{ print $1 }'); do
    [ "$(loginctl show-session "$session" -p Service --value 2>/dev/null)" = greetd ] || continue
    [ "$(loginctl show-session "$session" -p Class --value 2>/dev/null)" = user ] || continue
    presenting_by="session $session is signed in through it"
    return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# A settle window opens when greetd is active and something is presenting, and
# closes the moment either stops or greetd's generation moves. A window that
# stays open for SETTLE seconds makes a good boot. A window may open up to the
# deadline and then runs its full length; none opens after it, so a greeter
# that keeps coming and going is judged by the deadline.
window_start=""
window_generation=""
presented=no
while :; do
  now="$(date +%s)"
  if systemctl is-active --quiet greetd.service && greeter_presenting; then
    presented=yes
    generation="$(greetd_generation)"
    if [ -n "$window_start" ] && [ "$generation" = "$window_generation" ]; then
      if [ $(( now - window_start )) -ge "$SETTLE" ]; then
        log "greetd held its greeter for ${SETTLE}s without a restart"
        await_input
      fi
    else
      if [ -n "$window_start" ]; then
        warn "greetd was restarted while its greeter was presenting"
      fi
      window_start=""
      [ "$now" -ge "$end" ] && break
      window_start="$now"
      window_generation="$generation"
      log "greetd is active and $presenting_by (restarts so far: $(greetd_restarts)); waiting ${SETTLE}s for it to hold"
    fi
  else
    if [ -n "$window_start" ]; then
      warn "the greeter stopped presenting $(( now - window_start ))s into the ${SETTLE}s wait"
    fi
    window_start=""
    systemctl is-failed --quiet greetd.service && judge_failed
    [ "$now" -ge "$end" ] && break
  fi
  sleep "$POLL"
done

if [ "$presented" = yes ]; then
  warn "the greeter did not hold for ${SETTLE}s within ${DEADLINE}s: it went away again"
  warn "after presenting (greetd restarts: $(greetd_restarts)), so the screen keeps going"
  warn "black. This boot does not count as a success. State was:"
elif systemctl is-active --quiet greetd.service; then
  warn "greetd is active, but no greeter presented a frame within ${DEADLINE}s (greetd"
  warn "restarts: $(greetd_restarts)): there is nothing on the screen to log in at. This"
  warn "boot does not count as a success. State was:"
else
  warn "greetd.service did not become active within ${DEADLINE}s. This boot does not"
  warn "count as a success. State was:"
fi
final_state
exit 1
